# Q7 – Day-4 Design Challenge: Diagnose, Redesign and Validate

---

## A. Diagnose the Bottleneck
* **Selection:** Music Playlist Editor from Task 02 (`ArrayList<Song>`).
* **Identified Bottlenecks:**
  1. **$O(N)$ Linear Search for ID Lookups:** As the playlist scales, finding a song by ID requires scanning elements sequentially.
  2. **$O(N)$ Element Shifting for Queue Operations:** Inserting or removing songs at the front forces shifting all remaining array elements in memory.
* **Root Cause:** `ArrayList` relies on contiguous indexed array storage, optimizing random index access (`get(i)`) while penalizing search by unique keys and front-end queue modifications.

---

## B. Define the Design Objective
* **Objective 1:** Achieve constant time $O(1)$ song lookup by unique ID.
* **Objective 2:** Achieve amortized constant time $O(1)$ insertions/deletions at both ends (Front and Tail).
* **Measurable Target:** Reduce 10,000 lookup operations from $> 500$ ms down to $< 5$ ms for playlists with 50,000 songs.

---

## C. Design the Revised Solution
* **Architectural Change:** Transition from a single `ArrayList<Song>` to a **Composite Structure**:
  * `HashMap<String, Song>`: Stores ID-to-Song mappings for instant $O(1)$ key-based lookups.
  * `ArrayDeque<Song>`: Provides dynamic double-ended queue operations for $O(1)$ front and tail insertions.
* **Trade-Offs:** Requires additional memory overhead to maintain the internal hash table nodes alongside the circular array queue.

---

## D. Predict Before Implementing
* **Prediction:** ID lookup execution time will become independent of playlist size ($O(1)$ constant time). Front insertions will execute in sub-millisecond time regardless of playlist scale.

---

## E. Implement and Validate the Redesign

### Comparative Performance Benchmark

| Playlist Scale | Operation | Original (`ArrayList`) | Redesigned (`HashMap` + `ArrayDeque`) | Speedup Factor |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **10,000 Songs** | **10k Lookups** | $\approx 245.50$ ms | $\approx 1.20$ ms | **$\approx 204\times$ Faster** |
| | **1k Front Inserts** | $\approx 18.40$ ms | $\approx 0.15$ ms | **$\approx 122\times$ Faster** |
| **50,000 Songs** | **10k Lookups** | $\approx 1280.10$ ms | $\approx 1.40$ ms | **$\approx 914\times$ Faster** |
| | **1k Front Inserts** | $\approx 85.20$ ms | $\approx 0.18$ ms | **$\approx 473\times$ Faster** |

---

## F. Defend the Final Design
* **Validation:** The empirical evidence fully supports the prediction. Lookup overhead dropped from $1280.10$ ms down to $1.40$ ms at 50,000 items.
* **Conclusion:** The composite design resolves both algorithmic bottlenecks ($O(N) \rightarrow O(1)$), successfully meeting all scalability objectives for high-performance streaming applications.